## The completion

**"Perfection exists to improve the reliability with which a compiled artifact executes correctly on its intended inputs, unsupervised, on the first run."**

Execution reliability. Not completeness, not quality, not robustness. The reason is structural, not preferential:

**Three of your four candidates are monotonic in questions asked. Only one can be satisfied.**

Every question strictly increases specification completeness. Every question strictly increases robustness (asymptotically). Neither has an interior optimum, so neither yields a stopping rule — and an objective with no stopping rule *cannot* have a principled turn cap bolted onto it. Any cap you add is arbitrary, which is presumably why §3.1 has none.

The missing turn cap is not an oversight. It is the correct behaviour for the objective the prompt is actually optimizing.

Execution reliability is different: it can be *hit*. A question that resolves no likely halt and changes no determination has cost and zero benefit. Reliability also penalizes gates — a gate that trips is a failed unsupervised run. So you get a genuine two-sided tradeoff with an interior optimum, which is exactly the thing you need to price interaction cost.

| Candidate | Whose property | Behaviour | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| Specification completeness | the spec | monotonic in questions; no optimum | **Mechanism, not objective.** This is the current de facto goal and the creep engine. |
| Output quality | the deliverable | mostly not question-responsive; §16.3 disclaims detection | **Bounded by the rubric.** User-supplied. Perfection's only lever is eliciting rubric + reviewer. |
| Prompt robustness | the artifact | monotonic; scoped to *undeclared* inputs | **A dial, deliberately set low.** §16.3 already accepts this trade. It is what justifies §7.6-class nesting, and it should not. |
| **Execution reliability** | the run | two-sided; satisfiable; scoped to *declared* inputs | **The objective.** |

The clean line between the last two: reliability is scoped to the inputs the user declared; robustness is scoped to the ones they didn't. Perfection should own the first and explicitly disclaim the second — which it already does, in §16.3, and then contradicts in §7.

**One hard constraint, or the objective gets gamed.** "Reliability" must not be read as "minimize halts," or it licenses inventing values to keep the artifact running. Correctness is inside the definition: a halt is always preferable to a silent wrong determination. §9's no-invention rule stays a constraint, not a term in the tradeoff.

---

## The rule you asked for

It falls out of what a gate physically is.

**A gate is a hole of known shape. Gates can supply values. Gates cannot supply structure.**

To emit a gate you must already know the parameter's name, type, and the operation that consumes it. If you know that, the gate is a complete substitute for asking — it defers the value to the moment the user has the best information, at zero compile-time cost. If you *don't* know it — how many rubric dimensions exist, whether a reviewer exists at all, whether this workflow has a send-back branch — then no gate can be written, because the artifact would have to be a *different artifact*. That is the irreducible asymmetry, and it is the whole rule.

**Ask only if all three hold:**

1. **Structural** — the answer changes the artifact's shape (sections, workflow steps, rubric dimensions, active passes), not just a slot's contents.
2. **Reachable** — the parameter is consumed on a typical run, not only in a rare branch.
3. **Abstractable** — the user can answer correctly without a specific case in hand.

Fail any one → gate it, scoped per §4's `ON_MISSING`.

### What this reclassifies

| Parameter | Current | Under the rule |
|---|---|---|
| Materiality / tolerance threshold | Tier 1, ask-or-gate | **Gate.** Known shape, missing value. (§17.1 already does this.) |
| Rubric dimensions, adjudicative | Tier 1, "question it, or gate it" | **Ask.** Shape unknown; Rubric section and Pass B depend on it. Gating yields a no-op artifact. |
| Reviewer refusals + authority | ask (correct) | **Ask.** Pass B's entire content is this. |
| Reviewer precedence | Tier 1, never defaulted | **Gate, scoped.** An ordering over a named set is a value. |
| Residual precedence (§6.3) | Tier 1, blocking | **Gate.** Known shape, near-zero reachability. |
| Source tiers, ranked (§7.1) | Tier 1 | **Ask.** How many tiers is structural. |
| Conflict precedence (§7.2) | Tier 1 | **Gate**, once tiers are known. |
| Retrieval-failure behaviour (§7.5) | Tier 1 | **Ask** — a 2-option pick; it changes the Failure Protocol. |
| Concurrence substitution (§7.6) | Tier 2 w/ nested scope rules | **Ship the default line. Delete the rest.** |

Note that the no-invention guarantee is untouched throughout. Every item moved to the gate column is still never invented. What changes is *who bears the cost of the unknown, and when*.

---

## Slow mode survives, and now terminates on its own

You said Perfection was designed to participate in refinement. The rule vindicates that and explains why it kept running away:

**Refining structure terminates. Refining values does not.** Structural unknowns are enumerable — rubric shape, reviewer shape, source-tier shape, branch existence. You can exhaust them. Values are inexhaustible; there is always one more parameter. Slow mode has been doing both, so it inherited the unbounded one's behaviour.

Redefine it: **slow mode resolves structural unknowns only.** It ends when none remain. That is a natural stopping condition, not an arbitrary cap — which is the answer to the objection I'd have earned if I'd just proposed "cap it at three turns."

## Your other findings, re-derived

**Finding 2 — the cost model.** Replace it. Under reliability, a question failing any of the three tests is pure cost. And there's a compounding term the current model misses: question fatigue degrades answer quality on the questions that *do* matter. Over-asking therefore doesn't merely cost time — it costs accuracy on the material items. That makes over-classification a *reliability* failure, not a free safety margin, which is the argument the current sentence needs and lacks.

**Finding 3 — the checkpoint.** Its gag is a symptom. Under completeness, the checkpoint can't recommend stopping because completeness isn't reached, so all it may do is announce and repeat. Under reliability, the checkpoint *is* the satisfaction signal: no structural unknown remains, the objective is met. It should fire **once**, say so plainly, recommend compiling, and reduce questions to the remainder. Reporting a satisfied objective isn't editorializing.

**Finding 4 — provenance, and it gets cheaper.** Once values route to gates, Perfection wasn't going to re-ask most of them anyway. So the contract shrinks: Goal doesn't need per-field provenance on everything. It needs to mark **which structural choices the user actually made versus which Goal inferred.** That's a handful of fields, not a full mapping — a much easier thing to ask a closed engine for, and it eliminates the double-interview §15.1 currently mandates.

## What this doesn't fix

Reliability is scoped to declared inputs, so it gives you no purchase on the case where the user's declared scope was itself wrong. §16.3 already admits this and the admission should stay — but it belongs in one line, not six verbatim ones at the top of every artifact, competing for attention with the gates and ledger rows that are the actual product.
